{"name":"Breath of Green","hex":"#bdd1ce","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breath","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act or process of breathing.","A single act of breathing in or out; a breathing of air."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["voiceless, surd; contrasting with voice (breath sounds, voice sounds)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breath"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breathing","blocks":[{"text":"Breathing is the rhythmic process of inhaling air into the lungs providing oxygen, and exhaling the air removing carbon dioxide, in the gas exchange process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Real-time_MRI_-_Thorax.ogv/330px--Real-time_MRI_-_Thorax.ogv.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breathing"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg/500px-Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg/1280px-Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg/500px-Ribcage_during_inhalation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibcage_during_inhalation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Costillas_%28gray%29.png/330px-Costillas_%28gray%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Costillas_%28gray%29.png/330px-Costillas_%28gray%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACostillas_(gray).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Quiet_breathing.jpg/500px-Quiet_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Quiet_breathing.jpg/1280px-Quiet_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Quiet_breathing.jpg/500px-Quiet_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuiet_breathing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Forceful_breathing.jpg/500px-Forceful_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Forceful_breathing.jpg/1280px-Forceful_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Forceful_breathing.jpg/500px-Forceful_breathing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForceful_breathing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Nose_in_cold_weather.gif/500px-Nose_in_cold_weather.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Nose_in_cold_weather.gif/1280px-Nose_in_cold_weather.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Nose_in_cold_weather.gif/500px-Nose_in_cold_weather.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inhaled air is warmed and moistened by the wet, warm nasal mucosa, which consequently cools and dries. When warm, wet air from the lungs is breathed out through the nose, the cold hygroscopic mucus in the cool and dry nose re-captures some of the warmth and moisture from that exhaled air. In very cold weather the re-captured water may cause a \"dripping nose\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANose_in_cold_weather.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Illu_quiz_lung05.jpg/250px-Illu_quiz_lung05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped